      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 11,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "5.1.4 CPU Space Cycle \n",
      "bbox": [
        48.95000076293945,
        329.8275146484375,
        173.482421875,
        345.07891845703125
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.09712301738678462,
        0.5082401126125682,
        0.3442111545138889,
        0.5317414120643182
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 12,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "A CPU space cycle, indicated when the function codes are all high, is a special processor \ncycle. Bits A 16-A 19 of the address bus identify eight types of CPU space cycles. Only the \ninterrupt acknowledge cycle, in which A16-A19 are high, applies to all the \nmicroprocessors described in this manual. The MC68010 defines an additional type of \nCPU space cycle, the breakpoint acknowledge cycle, in which A 16-A 19 are all low. Other \nconfigurations of A 16-A 19 are reserved by Motorola to define other types of CPU cycles \nused in other M68000 Family microprocessors. Figure 5-10 shows the encoding of CPU \nspace addresses. \n",
      "bbox": [
        48.85000228881836,
        348.2300109863281,
        435.2748107910156,
        439.9004211425781
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
